 ### iVR-GS
-iVR-GS is a method for real-time editing of transfer functions and lighting during neural volume rendering using editable and composable 3DGS primitives, overcoming the limitation of baked visual parameters [S8, unofficial]. Steps: 1. Compose multiple basic models trained on disjoint transfer function ranges. 2. Extend Gaussian primitives with normals and shading coefficients. 3. Incorporate Blinn-Phong reflection. 4. Enable real-time editing of color, opacity, and lighting at inference. 5. Perform automatic optimization to approximate reference images [S8, unofficial]. Pitfalls: Model size scales linearly with the number of basic scenes, reaching gigabyte-level for hundreds of transfer functions. Uses uniform viewpoint sampling which may be suboptimal. Requires redesigning lighting representation for models beyond Blinn-Phong [S8, unofficial]. Reconstruction quality: PSNR approximately 32–34 dB for novel view synthesis, 30–33 dB for relighting [S8, unofficial]. Compression: Vector quantization achieved 7.2× compression (6.2 MB vs. 44.8 MB) while maintaining accuracy within 0.06 dB [S8, unofficial].
+iVR-GS is a method for real-time editing of transfer functions and lighting during neural volume rendering using editable and composable 3DGS primitives, overcoming the limitation of baked visual parameters [S8, unofficial]. Steps: 1. Compose multiple basic models trained on disjoint transfer function ranges [S8, unofficial]. 2. Extend Gaussian primitives with normals and shading coefficients [S8, unofficial]. 3. Incorporate Blinn-Phong reflection. 4. Enable real-time editing of color, opacity, and lighting at inference [S8, unofficial]. 5. Perform automatic optimization to approximate reference images [S8, unofficial]. Pitfalls: Model size scales linearly with the number of basic scenes, reaching gigabyte-level for hundreds of transfer functions [S8, unofficial]. Uses uniform viewpoint sampling which may be suboptimal [S8, unofficial]. Requires redesigning lighting representation for models beyond Blinn-Phong [S8, unofficial]. Reconstruction quality: PSNR approximately 32–34 dB for novel view synthesis, 30–33 dB for relighting [S8, unofficial]. Compression: Vector quantization achieved 7.2× compression (6.2 MB vs. 44.8 MB) while maintaining accuracy within 0.06 dB [S8, unofficial].
 
