 ## Background
-Radiance-field optimization emerged as a dominant paradigm for novel view synthesis following the introduction of Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) by Mildenhall et al. in 2020 [S2]. Prior to this, image-based rendering relied on light-field sampling or explicit geometry reconstruction [S2]. NeRF demonstrated that a continuous volumetric function, optimized via gradient descent to minimize photometric error, could synthesize photorealistic images from sparse views [S1, unofficial][S5][S10, unofficial]. The field has since expanded to include explicit representations like voxel grids [4694-direct-voxel-grid-optimization.md] and Gaussian splats [1795-3d-gaussian-splatting.md], as well as applications in dynamic scenes [912-neural-volumes.md], medical imaging [S5], and non-optical domains like radar [8904-radar-fields.md].
+Radiance-field optimization emerged as a dominant paradigm for novel view synthesis following the introduction of Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) by Mildenhall et al. in 2021 [S2]. Prior to this, image-based rendering relied on light-field sampling or explicit geometry reconstruction [S2]. NeRF demonstrated that a continuous volumetric function, optimized via gradient descent to minimize photometric error, could synthesize photorealistic images from sparse views [S2]. The field has since expanded to include explicit representations like voxel grids [4694-direct-voxel-grid-optimization.md] and Gaussian splats [1795-3d-gaussian-splatting.md], as well as applications in dynamic scenes [912-neural-volumes.md], medical imaging [S5], and non-optical domains like radar [8904-radar-fields.md].
 
